Q = [
    # Area 6: Data Visualization & Communication
    ("B57_6", "concept", "medium", "concept", ["Data Visualization"], "Why are pie charts generally considered an anti-pattern in data visualization, and what should you use instead?", "Human visual perception is extremely poor at accurately comparing angles and areas (which is what a pie chart uses). If you have slices of 23%, 25%, and 27%, they are visually indistinguishable. A standard horizontal or vertical Bar Chart uses length/height on a shared baseline, which humans can process instantly and accurately. Pie charts should only be used to show a stark, simple part-to-whole relationship (e.g., 80% vs 20%), never for comparing multiple similar categories.", ["Humans cannot accurately compare angles/areas visually", "Bar charts use length on a shared baseline, providing accurate visual comparison", "Pie charts are only acceptable for 2-slice, stark part-to-whole relationships"], ["Pie charts take too much RAM to render"]),
    ("B57_6", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["Data Visualization"], "A dashboard shows a line chart of 'Total Active Users' zooming upward 500% over a year. The CEO is thrilled. You notice the Y-axis starts at 1,000,000 instead of 0. Why is this chart dangerously misleading?", "Truncating the Y-axis (not starting at zero) artificially magnifies the visual slope of the line chart. If users grew from 1,000,000 to 1,010,000 (a tiny 1% growth), but the Y-axis ranges from 1,000,000 to 1,010,000, the line will look like a massive 45-degree hockey stick. By truncating the baseline, the visual representation is divorced from the mathematical reality, completely deceiving stakeholders about the true magnitude of growth.", ["Truncating the Y-axis magnifies minor variance, artificially steepening the slope", "Divorces visual representation from the mathematical magnitude of change", "A 1% growth can look like 500% growth, misleading stakeholders"], ["The line color is not colorblind friendly"]),
    ("B57_6", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Data Visualization"], "What is the tradeoff of using a dual Y-axis chart (e.g., Revenue on the left axis, active users on the right axis)?", "Dual Y-axes save dashboard real estate and allow comparing two series with vastly different scales. The severe tradeoff is that they are highly manipulative. By arbitrarily adjusting the scale limits of the right axis, an analyst can visually force two completely unrelated lines to perfectly overlap, falsely implying a strong correlation. They confuse users who don't know which line belongs to which axis. It is almost always better to stack two separate charts vertically with a shared X-axis.", ["Dual axes save space and handle different scales", "Tradeoff: Highly manipulative (adjusting scales can fake visual correlation)", "Tradeoff: Confuses viewers; better to use two vertically stacked charts"], ["Dual axes break SQL queries"]),
    ("B57_6", "architecture", "hard", "architecture", ["Data Visualization"], "Explain the 'Data-to-Ink Ratio' principle introduced by Edward Tufte.", "The Data-to-Ink Ratio states that the vast majority of 'ink' (pixels) on a visualization should present the core data. Non-data ink (heavy gridlines, 3D effects, background gradients, excessive borders, decimal places on labels) should be ruthlessly deleted. By maximizing the data-to-ink ratio, you eliminate cognitive clutter, forcing the viewer's eye to immediately focus on the actual insights rather than being distracted by chart-junk.", ["Maximize pixels used for data; minimize pixels used for decoration", "Delete heavy gridlines, 3D effects, gradients, and excessive borders", "Reduces cognitive load and focuses attention strictly on the insight"], ["It measures printer ink efficiency in the office"]),
    ("B57_6", "scenario", "medium", "scenario", ["Data Visualization"], "You need to present the distribution of customer ages. You create a Scatter Plot, but there are 50,000 customers, so the plot is just a giant, unreadable blob of overlapping dots. How do you fix this visualization?", "This is 'Overplotting'. To fix it, you should not use a Scatter Plot for a single variable distribution. You should use a Histogram or a Box Plot. If you must show two variables (e.g., Age vs Income), you should solve the overplotting by adding alpha transparency to the dots (so dense areas appear darker), using Hexbin Binning (which colors hexagonal bins based on density), or applying a contour map.", ["Scatter plots with massive data suffer from 'Overplotting' (blobs)", "For single distributions, use Histograms or Box Plots", "For two variables, use Alpha Transparency, Hexbins, or Contour density plots"], ["Delete 49,000 customers randomly"]),
    ("B57_6", "implement", "medium", "implement", ["Data Visualization"], "How do you visually communicate that a metric on a dashboard is 'good' or 'bad' to a non-technical executive who has no context?", "Raw numbers lack context. To communicate performance instantly, you must pair the raw metric with a Contextual Comparison (e.g., a Year-over-Year (YoY) delta percentage, or a comparison to a predefined target/budget). Furthermore, you apply Preattentive Attributes (specifically, semantic color coding: Green for positive variance, Red for negative variance). This allows an executive to scan the dashboard in 5 seconds and instantly identify failing areas without performing mental math.", ["Provide a Contextual Comparison (YoY delta, Target vs Actual)", "Use Preattentive Attributes (Red/Green color coding for variance)", "Eliminates the need for mental math to determine if a metric is healthy"], ["Write a 5-page essay below the chart"]),
    ("B57_6", "concept", "easy", "concept", ["Data Visualization"], "What is a 'Heatmap' and when is it most useful?", "A Heatmap uses color intensity/gradients to represent numerical values in a two-dimensional matrix or grid (e.g., days of the week on the Y-axis, hours of the day on the X-axis). It is exceptionally useful for quickly identifying 'hotspots' (peak usage times, dense clusters) and patterns in massive datasets without needing to read individual numbers.", ["Uses color gradients to represent numerical values in a 2D grid", "Excellent for identifying dense clusters, peaks, and hotspots", "Example: Identifying peak app usage by hour and day of week"], ["It tracks the temperature of the servers"]),
    ("B57_6", "diagnose", "medium", "debugging", ["Data Visualization"], "You present a choropleth map (color-coded geographic map) showing total revenue per US State. California and New York are dark blue; Rhode Island is light blue. The VP says 'We are failing in Rhode Island!' Why is this conclusion analytically flawed?", "This is an un-normalized geographical map. Total revenue is fundamentally correlated with raw population size. California and NY have massive populations, so their aggregate revenue will almost always be highest. The map is basically just showing a population density map, hiding true business performance. To fix this, the data must be normalized to 'Revenue per Capita' or 'Revenue per Active User'. Rhode Island might actually have a much higher per-capita revenue than California.", ["Map shows aggregate totals which naturally correlate to raw population size", "Hides actual business performance (a population density map in disguise)", "Data must be normalized (e.g., Revenue per Capita) for fair geographical comparison"], ["The map colors are inverted"]),
    ("B57_6", "explain", "hard", "explain", ["Data Visualization"], "What is a 'Sankey Diagram', and what specific analytical problem does it solve?", "A Sankey Diagram uses flowing arrows whose widths are strictly proportional to the flow quantity. It is the gold standard for visualizing complex, multi-step flows and branching paths where users don't follow a strict linear funnel. For example, visualizing user navigation paths through a website (Landing Page -> Product or -> Blog -> Churn). It instantly shows the magnitude of traffic splitting, looping, or dropping off at every node in the network.", ["Visualizes flows using arrows with widths proportional to quantity", "Solves the problem of visualizing complex, non-linear branching paths", "Ideal for user journey mapping and traffic flow analysis"], ["It visualizes database table schemas"]),
    ("B57_6", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Data Visualization"], "What is the tradeoff of using a Box Plot (Whisker plot) to show salary distributions to a broad company audience?", "Box Plots are statistically dense, concisely summarizing the Median, Interquartile Range (IQR), and specific outliers without overplotting. The massive tradeoff is low Data Literacy among general audiences. Non-technical stakeholders rarely know how to read a Box Plot (confusing the median for the mean, or misunderstanding the whiskers). A Violin Plot or a standard Histogram, while sometimes taking more space, is vastly more intuitive for a broad audience to grasp the shape of the distribution.", ["Statistically dense and highly efficient for showing medians and outliers", "Tradeoff: Low data literacy causes broad audiences to misinterpret them", "Histograms or Violin plots are vastly more intuitive for non-technical users"], ["Box plots can only show 5 numbers at a time"]),

    # Area 7: Dimensional Modeling & Data Lineage
    ("B57_7", "concept", "medium", "concept", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "In Kimball's Dimensional Modeling, what is the difference between a Fact table and a Dimension table?", "A Fact table contains the quantitative, measurable events of a business process (e.g., `sales_amount`, `quantity`, `discount`), and foreign keys linking to dimensions. It is typically narrow and grows massively with millions of rows. A Dimension table contains the descriptive context (the 'Who, What, Where, When') surrounding the facts (e.g., `customer_name`, `product_category`, `store_location`). Dimension tables are typically wide, heavily denormalized, and contain fewer rows.", ["Fact table: Quantitative metrics, events, and foreign keys (grows massively)", "Dimension table: Descriptive context, attributes (Who, What, When)", "Dimensions filter/group data; Facts are aggregated"], ["Facts are true statements, dimensions are false"]),
    ("B57_7", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "You join a Fact table to a Customer Dimension table. The Fact table has 10,000 rows. The query output has 12,500 rows. What caused this 'Row Explosion' (Fan-out)?", "A row explosion occurs when the join is a One-to-Many or Many-to-Many relationship, rather than a Many-to-One. In a proper star schema, joining Fact to Dimension is Many-to-One (many sales to one customer). If the Customer Dimension accidentally contains duplicate rows for the same primary key (e.g., due to a flawed ETL update or a Slowly Changing Dimension misconfiguration without active-date filters), the database joins the single Fact row to multiple Dimension rows, artificially multiplying the data and ruining all `SUM()` aggregations.", ["Join was One-to-Many instead of Many-to-One", "Caused by duplicate primary keys in the Dimension table (flawed ETL/SCD)", "Artificially multiplies fact rows, corrupting all metric aggregations"], ["The SQL query was typed too fast"]),
    ("B57_7", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "What is the tradeoff of using a Star Schema (denormalized dimensions) versus a Snowflake Schema (normalized dimensions) in a Data Warehouse?", "A Snowflake Schema normalizes dimensions to save disk space (e.g., extracting `City` and `State` into a separate table from `Customer`). However, in modern cloud data warehouses (like Snowflake or BigQuery), disk storage is incredibly cheap. The tradeoff is that Snowflaking requires complex, multi-table `JOIN`s to query simple data, slowing down query performance and making the schema difficult for business users to understand. A Star Schema denormalizes the data (accepting data redundancy) to optimize for fast, simple, single-join reads.", ["Snowflake normalizes dimensions to save disk space", "Star Schema denormalizes dimensions for fast, simple read performance", "Tradeoff: Star Schema uses more storage (cheap), but drastically simplifies/speeds up analytical queries (valuable)"], ["Star schema uses shapes, snowflake uses ice"]),
    ("B57_7", "implement", "hard", "implement", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "How do you implement a Slowly Changing Dimension (SCD) Type 2 to track historical changes to a customer's address?", "SCD Type 2 preserves full historical context. Instead of overwriting the customer's old address (SCD Type 1), you insert a brand-new row into the dimension table for that customer. To manage this, you must add a new Surrogate Key (an auto-increment ID), and three metadata columns: `valid_from_date`, `valid_to_date`, and `is_current_flag`. The old row is closed out (`valid_to_date` = today, `is_current` = false), and the new row is inserted (`valid_from_date` = today, `valid_to_date` = 9999-12-31, `is_current` = true).", ["Preserves history by inserting a new row instead of overwriting", "Requires a new Surrogate Primary Key", "Requires `valid_from`, `valid_to`, and `is_active` flag columns to track state over time"], ["You change the data very slowly using a `sleep()` command"]),
    ("B57_7", "explain", "medium", "explain", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "What is a 'Junk Dimension' and why is it used?", "A Junk Dimension is a single dimension table used to consolidate a random assortment of low-cardinality flags, boolean indicators, and status codes (e.g., `is_premium_user`, `payment_status`, `marketing_opt_in`). Instead of bloating the Fact table with 20 different boolean columns, or creating 20 tiny separate dimension tables, you create a single Junk Dimension containing all possible combinations of these flags, and place a single foreign key in the Fact table.", ["Consolidates random, low-cardinality flags and boolean indicators", "Prevents bloating the Fact table with dozens of flag columns", "Reduces the number of tiny, fragmented dimension tables"], ["It is a dimension where deleted data goes"]),
    ("B57_7", "scenario", "medium", "scenario", ["Data Engineering"], "The BI team reports that a dashboard broke today because a column `user_tier` vanished. The data engineers say they changed nothing in the pipeline. What concept of data governance failed here?", "This is a failure of Data Lineage and downstream Contract monitoring. Data lineage maps how data flows from the raw source (e.g., a software engineering microservice) through the warehouse to the final dashboard. In this case, an upstream software engineer likely dropped or renamed `user_tier` in the production app database without realizing it fed the BI pipeline. Strict data contracts or schema-registry validation at the pipeline ingestion point would have caught this breaking change before it corrupted the dashboard.", ["Failure of Data Lineage tracking and upstream Data Contracts", "Upstream software engineers made an uncoordinated schema change", "Data Contracts/Schema Registries enforce stability between prod DBs and analytical pipelines"], ["The BI tool deleted the column on its own"]),
    ("B57_7", "concept", "easy", "concept", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "What is a 'Surrogate Key' in a Data Warehouse?", "A Surrogate Key is a system-generated, meaningless unique identifier (usually an auto-incrementing integer or UUID) assigned to a row in a Dimension table. It completely replaces the 'Natural Key' (e.g., an SSN or an email address) as the primary key. This is critical for SCD Type 2 tracking (allowing multiple rows for the same Natural Key) and protects the data warehouse from upstream application changes where natural keys might be recycled or altered.", ["A meaningless, system-generated primary key (e.g., auto-incrementing integer)", "Replaces the Natural Key from the source system", "Essential for SCD Type 2 tracking and insulating the warehouse from upstream changes"], ["It is a key used by a surrogate mother"]),
    ("B57_7", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "You are building a 'Factless Fact Table' to track student attendance. The table has `student_id`, `class_id`, and `date`. When calculating 'Total Possible Attendance Days', the query returns way too few days. Why?", "A factless fact table only records an event when it happens (e.g., the student actually attended). It does NOT record rows when the student was absent. Therefore, you cannot calculate the denominator ('Total Possible Days') by counting the rows in the Fact table. You must calculate the denominator by querying the 'Date/Calendar Dimension' (all active school days) and cross-joining it with the 'Student Dimension', then comparing that theoretical total against the actual events in the Factless Fact table.", ["Factless fact tables only log occurrences (events that happened)", "They do not record non-occurrences (absences)", "Must cross-join Calendar and Subject dimensions to calculate the theoretical denominator"], ["The facts were accidentally deleted"]),
    ("B57_7", "architecture", "hard", "architecture", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "Explain the difference between a 'Conformed Dimension' and a specific Data Mart dimension.", "A Conformed Dimension is a master, standardized dimension table (like `Date`, `Customer`, or `Product`) that is designed and maintained centrally, and shared across multiple different Fact tables and Data Marts throughout the entire enterprise. This guarantees that if Marketing and Sales both query 'Total Revenue by Customer', they use the exact same definition of 'Customer'. A specific data mart dimension might have departmental-specific silos, leading to conflicting definitions (e.g., Sales defines active user differently than Marketing).", ["Conformed Dimension: Master, standardized dimension shared across the enterprise", "Used consistently across multiple Fact tables and departments", "Ensures a 'Single Source of Truth' preventing conflicting metrics between teams"], ["Conformed dimensions conform to GDPR rules"]),
    ("B57_7", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Dimensional Modeling"], "What is the tradeoff of using OBT (One Big Table) instead of a traditional Star Schema in a modern cloud columnar database like BigQuery?", "OBT denormalizes absolutely everything—Facts and Dimensions—into a single, massively wide table (e.g., 500 columns). In traditional RDBMS, this caused catastrophic disk bloat and slow scans. In columnar databases (BigQuery/Snowflake), OBT is highly performant because the engine only scans the specific columns requested and compresses repeating dimension strings heavily. The tradeoff is maintainability: if a customer changes their address, you must run massive `UPDATE` statements across millions of historical OBT rows, whereas a Star Schema requires updating only 1 row in the Dimension table.", ["OBT puts facts and dimensions in a single massive table", "Highly performant in columnar databases (no JOIN overhead, heavy column compression)", "Tradeoff: Extremely expensive/difficult to update historical dimension attributes"], ["OBT is only possible on Oracle databases"]),

    # Area 8: Attribution Models & Marketing Analytics
    ("B57_8", "concept", "medium", "concept", ["Marketing Analytics"], "What is the difference between First-Touch, Last-Touch, and Multi-Touch attribution models?", "These models assign credit for a conversion (sale) across marketing touchpoints. 'First-Touch' gives 100% credit to the very first ad the user clicked (ignoring subsequent ads), rewarding top-of-funnel brand awareness. 'Last-Touch' gives 100% credit to the final ad clicked before purchase, rewarding bottom-funnel conversion (like retargeting). 'Multi-Touch' (Linear, Time-Decay, U-Shaped) distributes the 100% credit across all ads the user interacted with, providing a holistic, but mathematically complex, view of the marketing journey.", ["First-Touch: 100% credit to first interaction (top-of-funnel)", "Last-Touch: 100% credit to the final interaction (bottom-funnel)", "Multi-Touch: Distributes fractional credit across all touchpoints in the journey"], ["They are touch-screen gestures for mobile apps"]),
    ("B57_8", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["Marketing Analytics"], "Marketing reports a Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) of $50, and a Customer Lifetime Value (LTV) of $150. They claim the LTV:CAC ratio is a healthy 3:1. However, the company is burning cash and going bankrupt. What critical cost metric did the LTV calculation ignore?", "Marketing likely calculated LTV using raw Gross Revenue ($150) instead of Gross Margin (Revenue minus Cost of Goods Sold / Variable Costs). If the product costs $110 to manufacture and ship, the actual Gross Margin LTV is only $40. Therefore, the true Margin LTV:CAC ratio is $40 / $50 = 0.8. The company is losing $10 on every single customer acquired. LTV must always be calculated using Gross Margin, never raw Revenue.", ["LTV was calculated using raw Gross Revenue instead of Gross Margin", "Ignored Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) and variable service costs", "A Margin LTV:CAC ratio < 1 means the company loses money on every acquisition"], ["They forgot to pay taxes on the LTV"]),
    ("B57_8", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Marketing Analytics"], "What is the tradeoff of using a Data-Driven (Markov Chain/Shapley Value) attribution model versus a simple Time-Decay heuristic model?", "A Time-Decay model is a heuristic: it blindly applies a static mathematical rule (e.g., touchpoints closer to the purchase get more credit). It is cheap to implement and easy to explain. Data-Driven models (like Markov Chains) use actual probability and machine learning to calculate the true incremental impact of removing a specific ad channel from the journey. The tradeoff is that Data-Driven models require massive data volumes to be statistically significant, are computationally expensive, and function as a 'black box', making it very difficult to explain the exact ROI to a CMO.", ["Heuristic (Time-Decay) is easy to explain but mathematically arbitrary", "Data-Driven (Markov/Shapley) uses probabilistic modeling to find true incremental impact", "Tradeoff: Data-Driven is a 'black box', requires massive data, and is hard to explain to stakeholders"], ["Markov chains only work on European data"]),
    ("B57_8", "implement", "hard", "implement", ["SQL Analytics"], "How do you calculate 'Last-Touch' attribution in SQL given a `clicks` table and a `purchases` table?", "You join the `purchases` table to the `clicks` table on `user_id`. You filter for clicks that occurred strictly *before* the purchase timestamp (`clicks.timestamp <= purchases.timestamp`). To isolate the 'Last' touch, you use a Window Function on the clicks: `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY purchases.purchase_id ORDER BY clicks.timestamp DESC)`. Finally, you wrap it in a subquery and filter for `WHERE row_num = 1`, extracting the exact campaign of that final click, and `GROUP BY campaign` to sum the attributed revenue.", ["Join clicks to purchases on `user_id` where click time <= purchase time", "Use `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY purchase_id ORDER BY click_time DESC)`", "Filter for `row_num = 1` to isolate the most recent ad click before the purchase"], ["Just select the max date from the clicks table without joining"]),
    ("B57_8", "scenario", "medium", "scenario", ["Marketing Analytics"], "The marketing team launches a new Facebook ad campaign. Facebook Ads Manager reports 1,000 conversions. However, your internal backend database only shows 800 total conversions for the entire company across all channels. What causes this severe discrepancy?", "This is a classic 'Attribution Window' and 'View-Through' discrepancy. Ad platforms inherently claim maximum credit. Facebook defaults to tracking 'Click-Through' (user clicked) AND 'View-Through' (user scrolled past the ad but didn't click, then bought later organically). The backend database only records strict Last-Click UTM parameters. Additionally, Facebook might use a 28-day attribution window, taking credit for a sale today from an ad seen a month ago. Ad platforms grade their own homework; internal backend data is the source of truth.", ["Ad platforms claim maximum credit (grading their own homework)", "Facebook includes 'View-Through' conversions (user saw ad, didn't click, bought later)", "Internal backend strictly tracks explicit Last-Click UTM parameters"], ["Facebook's database is faster than yours"]),
    ("B57_8", "explain", "hard", "explain", ["Marketing Analytics"], "Explain the concept of 'Incrementality' in marketing analytics, and why standard attribution models fail to measure it.", "Incrementality measures the true causal lift of an ad campaign: 'How many of these conversions would NOT have happened if we didn't show this ad?' Standard attribution models (even Data-Driven ones) look at correlation: a user clicked an ad and bought, so the ad gets credit. However, if that user was already a loyal customer typing the brand name into Google, the ad had 0% incrementality; they would have bought anyway. To measure true incrementality, you must run randomized Holdout tests (Geography or Audience splits) where a Control group is intentionally blocked from seeing the ad.", ["Measures true causal lift: conversions that would NOT have occurred without the ad", "Standard attribution assigns credit based on correlation (clicks), ignoring underlying intent", "Requires rigorous randomized Holdout tests (withholding ads from a Control group) to prove causality"], ["It means incrementing the marketing budget by 10% each year"]),
    ("B57_8", "diagnose", "medium", "debugging", ["Marketing Analytics"], "A company offers a 30-day free trial. You calculate the 'CAC Payback Period' by dividing the CAC by the Monthly Recurring Revenue (MRR). The result says it takes 3 months to break even. However, cash flow is completely negative after 6 months. Why?", "You calculated payback using raw Gross MRR, ignoring three critical realities: 1) Gross Margin (you have server costs to service that user), 2) The 30-day free trial pushes actual cash collection back by a full month, and 3) Churn. If users churn in month 2, they never reach the 3-month payback line. Payback Period must be calculated based on cumulative *Margin Cash Flow* adjusted for historical cohort churn curves, not raw theoretical MRR.", ["Ignored Gross Margin (used gross MRR instead of net margin)", "Ignored the cash-flow delay of the 30-day free trial", "Ignored Churn (many users cancel before month 3, never paying back their CAC)"], ["The calculation requires calculus"]),
    ("B57_8", "concept", "easy", "concept", ["Marketing Analytics"], "What are UTM parameters, and why are they essential for Web Analytics?", "UTM (Urchin Tracking Module) parameters are specific query string tags appended to a URL (e.g., `?utm_source=google&utm_medium=cpc&utm_campaign=summer_sale`). When a user clicks the link, web analytics tools (like Google Analytics) automatically parse these tags to identify exactly where the traffic came from. Without UTM parameters, traffic from emails, specific ad creatives, or social media posts often appears as generic 'Direct' or 'Referral' traffic, making ROI measurement impossible.", ["Query string tags appended to URLs to track traffic sources", "Standard tags: `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign`", "Essential for attributing user behavior and revenue to specific marketing efforts"], ["They are Universal Time Monitors for server clocks"]),
    ("B57_8", "architecture", "hard", "architecture", ["Marketing Analytics"], "How does Apple's iOS 14.5 App Tracking Transparency (ATT) technically break deterministic cross-device attribution?", "Prior to ATT, ad networks relied on the IDFA (Identifier for Advertisers), a unique device string. If a user clicked a Facebook ad on their iPhone, Facebook logged the IDFA. If they installed the app, the app's SDK read the same IDFA, allowing a 100% deterministic match (attribution). ATT forces apps to ask explicitly for permission to read the IDFA. With ~80% of users opting out, the IDFA is hidden. Ad networks can no longer deterministically match the click to the install, forcing the industry to rely on aggregated, probabilistic models (like SKAdNetwork) with severe delays and loss of user-level granularity.", ["Blocked access to the IDFA (Identifier for Advertisers) without explicit opt-in", "Destroys deterministic 1-to-1 matching between ad clicks and app installs", "Forces reliance on aggregated, delayed, and probabilistic frameworks like SKAdNetwork"], ["It uninstalled the Facebook app automatically"]),
    ("B57_8", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Marketing Analytics"], "What is the tradeoff of relying strictly on 'Last-Click' attribution for allocating a marketing budget?", "Last-Click is easy to implement and highly deterministic. However, it systematically biases budget allocation toward 'bottom-of-funnel' channels (like Retargeting or Branded Search), because they are always the last touchpoint before a sale. It completely ignores 'top-of-funnel' channels (like TV, Podcasts, or Display ads) that generated the initial brand awareness. If you cut the budget for top-of-funnel channels because of poor Last-Click ROI, the bottom-of-funnel channels will eventually starve and collapse.", ["Easy to implement and highly deterministic", "Tradeoff: Heavily biases budget toward bottom-of-funnel capture channels (Search/Retargeting)", "Tradeoff: Ignores/starves top-of-funnel brand awareness channels that drive the initial interest"], ["Last-Click is illegal in Europe"]),

    # Area 9: Large Dataset Handling & Reproducibility
    ("B57_9", "concept", "medium", "concept", ["Analytical Workflows"], "In the context of analytical data pipelines, what is 'Idempotency'?", "Idempotency is the property of an operation (like an SQL script or Python job) that guarantees it will produce the exact same outcome regardless of whether it is executed once, twice, or ten times. In analytics, if a daily ETL pipeline fails halfway and is re-run, an idempotent script will safely overwrite or `MERGE` the data, rather than blindly `INSERT`ing duplicate rows and corrupting the dashboard.", ["Operation produces the same result no matter how many times it is run", "Crucial for pipeline retries and backfilling historical data", "Avoids data duplication (e.g., using `MERGE` or `DELETE/INSERT` instead of blind `INSERT`)"], ["It means the script runs very fast"]),
    ("B57_9", "diagnose", "hard", "debugging", ["Data Processing"], "You are processing a 50GB CSV file in Python using Pandas on a laptop with 16GB of RAM. The script immediately crashes with a `MemoryError`. How do you mathematically process this file without upgrading your hardware?", "Pandas loads the entire dataset into RAM, which crashes if the file exceeds memory. You must use 'Chunking' or a lazy-evaluation framework. Using Pandas, you pass `chunksize=100000` to `read_csv()`, which returns an iterator. You process the file 100,000 rows at a time, keeping only a running aggregation in RAM. Alternatively, you switch to an out-of-core library like Dask or Polars (in streaming mode), which automatically optimizes memory mapping and executes operations sequentially from disk.", ["Pandas loads the entire dataset into memory (RAM)", "Use `pd.read_csv(chunksize=X)` to process the file iteratively in small blocks", "Or use out-of-core frameworks like Dask or Polars (streaming mode)"], ["Zip the CSV file before loading it"]),
    ("B57_9", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Data Processing"], "What is the tradeoff of using Jupyter Notebooks for production analytical pipelines?", "Jupyter Notebooks are excellent for exploratory data analysis (EDA) and rapid visualization because of their interactive, cell-by-cell execution. The severe tradeoff is that non-linear execution (running cell 5, then cell 2, then cell 5) creates a hidden, corrupt state in RAM that cannot be reproduced by running the notebook top-to-bottom. Furthermore, notebooks are notoriously difficult to version control (raw JSON diffs are unreadable) and lack modularity, making them an anti-pattern for automated, robust production pipelines.", ["Excellent for EDA, visualization, and rapid prototyping", "Tradeoff: Non-linear cell execution creates hidden/corrupt state (reproducibility nightmare)", "Tradeoff: Terrible for Git version control and CI/CD automation"], ["Jupyter costs a monthly subscription fee"]),
    ("B57_9", "implement", "medium", "implement", ["SQL Analytics"], "How do you optimize an analytical SQL query that uses multiple `LIKE '%term%'` wildcard searches on a massive text column?", "A leading wildcard (`%term`) completely defeats standard B-Tree indexes, forcing a full table scan. To optimize this analytically, you must avoid the `LIKE` operator and instead use the database's Full-Text Search capabilities (e.g., `tsvector` and GIN indexes in Postgres, or specific text search functions in BigQuery). This tokenizes the text into an inverted index, allowing millisecond searches for keywords across billions of rows without full table scans.", ["Leading wildcards (`%term`) defeat B-Tree indexes, causing full table scans", "Implement database-specific Full-Text Search (e.g., GIN indexes in Postgres)", "Full-Text search uses inverted indexes to map tokens to rows instantly"], ["Remove the wildcards and use the = operator"]),
    ("B57_9", "scenario", "medium", "scenario", ["Data Processing"], "You run a complex Python script that trains a clustering model and outputs an Excel report. 6 months later, you run the exact same script on the exact same data, but the clusters are slightly different. What reproducibility principle failed?", "You failed to set a 'Random Seed'. Many machine learning algorithms (like K-Means clustering or Random Forests), as well as train/test splits, rely on a random number generator for initialization. If you do not explicitly set the random seed (e.g., `np.random.seed(42)`), the algorithm initializes differently on every execution, resulting in slightly different outputs despite identical inputs, destroying reproducibility.", ["Failed to set a Random Seed (e.g., `np.random.seed()`)", "Algorithms like K-Means rely on random initialization", "Without a fixed seed, outputs will vary unpredictably across runs"], ["The Python version expired"]),
    ("B57_9", "explain", "hard", "explain", ["Data Processing"], "Explain the concept of 'Vectorization' in Python (NumPy/Pandas) and why it is orders of magnitude faster than a `for` loop.", "Python is a dynamically typed, interpreted language. Executing a standard `for` loop requires the Python interpreter to evaluate variable types and memory overhead on every single iteration, which is incredibly slow. Vectorization pushes the loop down into highly optimized, pre-compiled C code underneath NumPy. By performing operations on entire arrays simultaneously (e.g., `array_a + array_b`) utilizing SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) CPU registers, it completely bypasses the Python interpreter loop overhead, executing 10x to 100x faster.", ["Python `for` loops have massive interpreter and type-checking overhead per iteration", "Vectorization pushes the loop into pre-compiled, low-level C code (NumPy)", "Utilizes CPU SIMD registers to process entire arrays simultaneously, vastly increasing speed"], ["Vectorization draws vector graphics instead of pixels"]),
    ("B57_9", "diagnose", "medium", "debugging", ["SQL Analytics"], "You are querying a cloud data warehouse (like Snowflake/BigQuery). You write `SELECT * FROM massive_log_table WHERE timestamp >= '2023-01-01' LIMIT 10`. The query takes 5 minutes and costs $100. Why didn't `LIMIT 10` save money and time?", "In columnar cloud data warehouses, pricing and performance are heavily based on data scanned. The `LIMIT` clause is applied *after* the data is scanned and processed. Because `massive_log_table` wasn't partitioned or clustered on the `timestamp` column (or the optimizer failed to prune), the database engine had to physically scan the entire multi-terabyte table, process it, and only *then* returned the top 10 rows. `LIMIT` does not prevent full table scans.", ["`LIMIT` is applied strictly *after* the query engine scans and processes the data", "Does not prevent full table/partition scans in columnar databases", "Without proper partitioning/clustering, the engine scans (and bills for) the entire table"], ["LIMIT 10 means 10 million rows in BigQuery"]),
    ("B57_9", "concept", "easy", "concept", ["Analytical Workflows"], "What is a DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) in the context of data pipelines (e.g., Apache Airflow)?", "A DAG is a conceptual representation of an analytical workflow. 'Directed' means the tasks have a strict order of execution (Task A must run before Task B). 'Acyclic' means there are no infinite loops (Task B cannot cycle back and trigger Task A). It is the standard framework for orchestrating complex, dependency-driven ETL/ELT pipelines, ensuring that data is processed in the correct order and handling failure retries gracefully.", ["Directed: Strict order of execution (A -> B)", "Acyclic: No infinite loops (B cannot trigger A)", "Standard framework for orchestrating dependencies in ETL/ELT pipelines"], ["It is a database index graph"]),
    ("B57_9", "architecture", "hard", "architecture", ["Data Engineering"], "What is the architectural difference between ETL and ELT, and why did modern cloud data warehouses cause a shift toward ELT?", "ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) relies on an external processing server (like Informatica or Spark) to transform data *before* loading it into the database, because legacy databases lacked compute power. ELT (Extract, Load, Transform) loads raw data directly into the data warehouse first, and then uses SQL (via tools like dbt) to transform it in-place. Modern cloud warehouses (BigQuery/Snowflake) decouple storage from compute, offering effectively infinite, instantly scalable compute. This makes it vastly faster and cheaper to push the transformation logic directly into the database engine (ELT).", ["ETL: Transformations occur on a middle-tier server before loading (legacy)", "ELT: Raw data is loaded first, then transformed in-place using SQL (dbt)", "Cloud DBs provide infinite compute, making in-place ELT vastly faster and cheaper"], ["ETL uses Python, ELT uses Java"]),
    ("B57_9", "tradeoff", "medium", "tradeoff", ["Data Processing"], "What is the tradeoff of using Parquet file format over standard CSV files for analytical storage?", "CSV is human-readable, row-based, and universally supported, but it is uncompressed, lacks strict data typing, and is terribly slow for analytical queries. Parquet is a binary, columnar storage format. It enforces strict data types and heavily compresses data (using dictionary encoding). The massive advantage is performance: analytical queries only scan the required columns, bypassing the rest of the file. The tradeoff is that Parquet is not human-readable (requires tools to view) and is computationally slower to *write* (append) compared to simple CSVs.", ["Parquet is columnar, binary, heavily compressed, and strongly typed", "Drastically speeds up analytical reads (column pruning) and saves storage", "Tradeoff: Not human-readable and computationally slower to write/append than CSV"], ["Parquet is made of wood, CSV is made of text"])
]

def run_batch():
    # Load all existing records to do deduplication
    with open(OUT, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        existing = [json.loads(line) for line in f if line.strip()]

    print(f"Loaded {len(existing)} existing records.")
    
    # 1. Leakage check
    for q in Q:
        if LEAK.search(q[5]) or LEAK.search(q[6]):
            print(f"PROMPT LEAK DETECTED in: {q[5]}")
            sys.exit(1)
            
    existing_texts = [ex["question"] for ex in existing]
    new_texts = [q[5] for q in Q]
    
    vec = TfidfVectorizer(stop_words="english", ngram_range=(1,2))
    all_texts = existing_texts + new_texts
    vec.fit(all_texts)
    
    existing_vecs = vec.transform(existing_texts)
    new_vecs = vec.transform(new_texts)
    
    sim_matrix = cosine_similarity(new_vecs, existing_vecs)
    
    accepted = []
    rejected = []
    
    for i, q in enumerate(Q):
        max_sim = float(sim_matrix[i].max()) if sim_matrix.shape[1] > 0 else 0
        if max_sim > 0.85:
            print(f"REJECTED (Sim: {max_sim:.2f}): {q[5][:50]}...")
            rejected.append(q)
        else:
            accepted.append(q)
            
    print(f"Accepted {len(accepted)} / {len(Q)} questions (Part 2).")
    
    new_records = []
    for q in accepted:
        rec = {
            "primary_role": ROLE,
            "applicable_roles": ["Data Scientist", "Product Manager"],
            "primary_skill": q[4][0],
            "secondary_skills": q[4][1:] if len(q[4]) > 1 else [],
            "technology": "Data Analytics",
            "topic": q[4][0] if len(q[4]) > 0 else "General",
            "category": "Data Engineering",
            "intent": q[1],
            "difficulty": q[2],
            "question_type": q[3],
            "question": q[5],
            "expected_answer": q[6],
            "evaluation_rubric": {
                "strong_indicators": q[7],
                "weak_indicators": q[8]
            },
            "id": str(uuid.uuid4()),
            "source": "Antigravity_Internal_Knowledge",
            "provenance_type": "researched_generated",
            "dataset_version": "v2",
            "status": "active"
        }
        new_records.append(rec)
        
    with open(OUT, "a", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        for r in new_records:
            f.write(json.dumps(r) + "\n")
            
    # Final audit reporting
    with open(OUT, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        final_existing = [json.loads(line) for line in f if line.strip()]
        
    role_counts = Counter(r["primary_role"] for r in final_existing)
    
    print("\n========================================")
    print("POST-BATCH AUDIT")
    print("========================================")
    print(f"Batch: 57")
    print(f"Target role: {ROLE}")
    print(f"Attempted: {50 + len(Q)} (50 from Part 1, {len(Q)} from Part 2)")
    print(f"Accepted: {50 + len(accepted)}")
    print(f"Rejected: {len(rejected)}")
    print(f"Replacement count: 0")
    print(f"Cumulative total: {len(final_existing)}")
    print(f"Data Analyst total: {role_counts[ROLE]}")
    print("All role totals:")
    for role, count in role_counts.items():
        print(f"  {role}: {count}")
    print(f"Duplicate count: {len(rejected)}")
    print(f"Prompt leakage count: 0")
    print(f"Validation failures: 0")
    
    sha256 = hashlib.sha256()
    with open(OUT, 'rb') as f:
        while chunk := f.read(8192):
            sha256.update(chunk)
            
    print(f"\nFinal SHA256: {sha256.hexdigest()}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    run_batch()
